mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12.0000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="仿宋"><p><span style="font-family: 'times new roman', times, serif;">Pathology is divided into general pathology and systemic pathology. General pathology elaborates on the common patterns of disease, including fundamental pathological changes such as cellular injury, inflammation, and neoplasia. Systemic pathology focuses on diseases of specific organ systems, with an emphasis on analyzing the etiology, mechanisms, pathological alterations, and clinical correlations of common disorders. The teaching approach prioritizes the integration of theory with practice, emphasizing the connection between morphology and function, the unity of local and systemic perspectives, and the incorporation of cutting-edge advancements in the field. This reflects the seamless integration of basic science with clinical medicine, showcasing the evolving standards of modern medical science.</span></p></font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
